Z||000000||RFMike||05-11-99||08:04 PM||||i posted a request for anyone with info on software to read error codes (under "its alive").  it may have gotten lost in the number of responses. this was a real live magazine review in the Computer Shopper by a competent gearhead for software and an interface cable for reading error codes on cars.  it was about two years ago( i think) when i read it.  i have enough spare computer parts laying around to put together a low end computer for my garage.  the price for the package was about $40, as i remember.  i have done some online searching, but can't find this package, or anything like it.  any suggestions?<br>ps- do fieros have more error code occurences than the everage gm product?  understandable if they did, with the amount of new ground broken at the time.
Z||000001||theogre||05-11-99||08:21 PM||||Acutally as far as engines go.... Fiero is far from Unique. These engines are used in a number of other GM cars. Cavalier, Cutless Cierra, and so on.<p>There are a few places you can find data about getting codes. the simple method is to use the check engine light. Jump 2 ALDL terminals turn on the key and count the blinks.<p>There is software for PC in development for free, as well as comercial product. here's one free one....  <A HREF="http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/fiero.htm</A> <p>You can also get an "AutoXRay" GM code scanner that covers 81-95 GM cars for about 150$us. nice neat small device that works quite well, and can monitor in real time. I have one. I like it. frankly free is nice, but only if it saves me money or signifigant amounts of time.... in this case free doesn't save my time enough to make it worth the effort.<p><b>Note</b> some computers can't feed data and be driven at the same time. This is a limit of the ECM and applies to all real time monitoring methods. My 874cyl for example can be monitored and driven, but it pushes up the idle about 100RPM when monitoring. Some cars can't be driven when monitoring or you could end up causing more problems, or getting false readings.
